<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1889 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Red Flag</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A socialist song of 1889 about the workers' red flag, sung at the close of the British and Irish Labour Party conferences.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jim Connell</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (usually sung to &quot;Lauriger Horatius&quot;, the tune of &quot;O Tannenbaum&quot;; Connell intended &quot;The White Cockade&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the international labour movement; the British Labour Party, the Irish Labour Party and the SDLP</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (written in Nicholas Donovan's house)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1889</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Jim Connell, written 1889; printed in the IWW's Little Red Songbook, 1909; tune of &quot;Lauriger Horatius&quot; / &quot;O Tannenbaum&quot; (traditional))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Irishman Jim Connell wrote &quot;The Red Flag&quot; in 1889 in Nicholas Donovan's house: six stanzas, each followed by a chorus, praising the sacrifices and solidarity of the international labour movement. Connell wanted it sung to &quot;The White Cockade&quot;, a pro-Jacobite song by Robert Burns, but in the 1890s the British socialist writer Adolphe Smith Headingley popularised it to &quot;Lauriger Horatius&quot;, the tune better known as the German carol &quot;O Tannenbaum&quot;. Connell disliked that tune, which he called church music and conservative by nature; it is the one usually sung today.</p><p>The song spread with the workers' movement. It was the first song in the first edition of the Industrial Workers of the World's Little Red Songbook in 1909, which printed five of its six stanzas. In 1913 the IWW songwriter Joe Hill, drawing on his time in the Magonista rebellion of 1911 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Lower California</a>, argued in the Industrial Worker that the song should speak of the workers rather than &quot;the people&quot;, whom he saw as middle class. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> the Runanga Band played it at the burial of Henry John Morris at Greymouth on 11 January 1920, as he had asked: he had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1908 to work in New Zealand's government mines and was prominent in the local socialist and labour movement.</p><p>It became the anthem of the British Labour Party from its founding, and its annual conference closes with it, along with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>&quot;; it is also the anthem of the Irish Labour Party and of Northern Ireland's Social Democratic and Labour Party, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Labour Party used it until the late 1940s. It was first sung in the House of Commons on 1 August 1945, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>'s Labour Party defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>'s Conservatives, and again on 27 May 1976, on the night of 28 March 1979 when a vote of no confidence brought down the Labour government, in February 2006 for the party's centenary and in September 2019 in protest at the prorogation of Parliament. In the 1950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> dockers sang it at the end of meetings with management while the managers in the gallery sang &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot;. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tony-blair">Tony Blair</a> the leadership was said to play it down; Jeremy Corbyn's supporters sang it when he was elected leader in 2015.</p><p>Translated versions are sung by the Japanese Communist Party and the Korean People's Army. The song has also been much reworked: parodied by Leon Rosselson, widely known from the 1960s, to satirise a perceived lack of socialist principles in the Labour Party, turned by football supporters into club chants, and recorded by Shakin Stevens and Robert Wyatt in 1982 and by Billy Bragg and Dick Gaughan in 1990, sung to Connell's own choice of tune.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song in honour of the red flag as the banner of the workers' movement and of the sacrifices made for it. It pledges loyalty to the cause through every hardship. Its mood is solemn and defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The People's Flag is deepest red,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It shrouded oft our martyred dead,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And ere their limbs grew stiff and cold,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their hearts' blood dyed its every fold.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then raise the scarlet standard high.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Beneath its shade we'll live and die,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Though cowards flinch and traitors sneer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll keep the red flag flying here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Look round, the Frenchman loves its blaze,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The sturdy German chants its praise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Moscow's vaults its hymns were sung</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chicago swells the surging throng.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>(chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It waved above our infant might,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When all ahead seemed dark as night;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It witnessed many a deed and vow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We must not change its colour now.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>(chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It well recalls the triumphs past,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It gives the hope of peace at last;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The banner bright, the symbol plain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of human right and human gain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>(chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>It suits today the weak and base,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whose minds are fixed on pelf and place</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To cringe before the rich man's frown,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And haul the sacred emblem down.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>(chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With head uncovered swear we all</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To bear it onward till we fall;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Come dungeons dark or gallows grim,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>This song shall be our parting hymn.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Jim Connell (1889). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Red_Flag" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="hKMtYv6kdCA" data-title="The Red Flag: Billy Bragg"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Billy Bragg</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Billy Bragg - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hKMtYv6kdCA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Little Red Songbook (Industrial Workers of the World)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1909</span><span class="sg-chain-h">printed in (five of six stanzas)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Through Bolshevik Russia (Ethel Snowden)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1920</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody printed in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The people's flag is palest pink (schoolchildren's version)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1950s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Battle Hymn of the New Socialist Party (Leon Rosselson)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Liberal Democrats' Glee Club version</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1960s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Foreman's Job</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Red Flag Rock (Shakin Stevens)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1982</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rock and roll cover</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nothing Can Stop Us (Robert Wyatt)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1982</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Billy Bragg and Dick Gaughan</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording, to the tune of &quot;The White Cockade&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rubens Has Been Shot (Chumbawamba)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We'll Never Die (Manchester United F.C.)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">football chant to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Blue Flag (Chelsea F.C. and Oldham Athletic supporters)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">football chant</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Michaelis Anthem (Harold Baum), The Biochemists' Songbook</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Net Flag</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Citizen Smith, television sitcom</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1970s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune used in the title sequence</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lauriger Horatius (O Tannenbaum)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune it is usually sung to</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The White Cockade (Robert Burns)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune Connell intended</span></li></ul></section>
